**Solution.**

Let $R$ be an associative ring with identity having $35$ elements. Since $35 = 5\cdot 7$ with $5,7$ distinct primes, the only abelian group of order $35$ is the cyclic one, so $(R,+) \cong \mathbb{Z}/35\mathbb{Z}$. Fix a generator $g$ of the additive group.

**Multiplication is determined by $g\cdot g$.** By bilinearity of multiplication over addition, every product is determined by $g\cdot g$:
$$(mg)\cdot(ng) = mn(g\cdot g) \quad\text{for all } m,n\in\mathbb{Z}.$$
Write $g\cdot g = sg$ for some $s\in\mathbb{Z}/35\mathbb{Z}$.

**Associativity is automatic.** We compute
$$((mg)\cdot(ng))\cdot(pg) = (mn\,sg)\cdot(pg) = mnp\,(sg\cdot g) = mnp\,s(g\cdot g) = (mnps)\,g,$$
$$(mg)\cdot((ng)\cdot(pg)) = (mg)\cdot(np\,sg) = mnp\,(g\cdot sg) = mnp\,s(g\cdot g) = (mnps)\,g.$$
These agree for all $m,n,p$, so the multiplication is associative (and commutative) for every choice of $s$.

**Identity exists iff $s$ is a unit mod $35$.** An element $e = tg$ is a multiplicative identity iff $e\cdot(mg) = mg$ for all $m$, i.e.
$$tm\,(g\cdot g) = mg \iff tms\,g = m\,g \iff ts\equiv 1 \pmod{35}.$$
Such $t$ exists iff $s$ is invertible modulo $35$, i.e. $\gcd(s,35)=1$. So the valid rings are parametrized by $s\in(\mathbb{Z}/35\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$.

**All such rings are isomorphic.** Consider two rings $R_s$ (with $g\cdot g = sg$) and $R_{s'}$ (with $g\cdot g = s'g$) on the same cyclic group. A group automorphism $\varphi(g)=ug$ (with $u$ a unit) is a ring isomorphism $R_s\to R_{s'}$ iff
$$\varphi(g\cdot_s g) = \varphi(g)\cdot_{s'}\varphi(g).$$
The left side is $\varphi(sg) = s\,ug = (su)g$. The right side is $(ug)\cdot_{s'}(ug) = u^{2}(g\cdot_{s'} g) = u^{2}s'\,g = (u^{2}s')g$. Equality requires $su = u^{2}s'$, i.e. $s' = s\,u^{-1}$. Since $s$ and $s'$ are both units, $s' s^{-1} = u^{-1}$ is a unit, so a suitable $u$ always exists. Hence every pair of valid parameters gives isomorphic rings.

Therefore there is exactly **one** isomorphism class, represented by the usual ring $\mathbb{Z}/35\mathbb{Z}$.

$$\boxed{1}$$

